% Test: guards that imply groundness allow guard-only reader occurrences
% Per spec: guard reader occurrences count toward SRSW IF the guard implies groundness.
% Guards that imply groundness: ground/1, integer/1, number/1, atom/1, string/1, etc.
% Guards that do NOT imply groundness: known/1 (bound but may contain unbound vars)

% ground/1 guard: implies groundness, so X? in guard satisfies SRSW
guard_ground(X) :- ground(X?) | true.

% integer/1 guard: implies groundness (integers are ground)
guard_int(X) :- integer(X?) | true.

% Arithmetic comparison guard: both X? and Y? are grounded (comparison requires ground numbers)
guard_compare(X, Y) :- X? < Y? | true.

% known/1 does NOT imply groundness - bound value may contain unbound vars
% So this would be an SRSW violation if X? only appears in guard:
% guard_known(X) :- known(X?) | true.  % INVALID - X has no grounded reader

% To use known/1 correctly, X? must also appear in body or with a grounding guard:
guard_known_valid(X, Y?) :- known(X?) | Y = X?.  % X? in body satisfies SRSW
